## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design runtime-extensible components with asynchronous rules and per-request isolation in a high-stakes fintech domain.

## Key Concepts
- **Runtime registries** for dynamic strategy extension without code changes
- **Async/sync rule composition** with uniform error aggregation across all rule types
- **Per-request state isolation** ensuring safe parallelization and preventing cross-contamination between transactions
- **Trade-offs: Pipeline vs Chain of Responsibility**
  - *Pipeline*: Centralized orchestration, easy to add/remove stages, simpler error aggregation, but rules have less control over flow
  - *Chain of Responsibility*: Rules can decide to pass or halt, more flexible branching logic, but harder to reorder dynamically and error handling is distributed
  - *Our choice*: Pipeline provides better runtime extensibility and uniform error collection—critical for compliance reporting in fintech.

## Code Example
```typescript
interface PaymentRequest {
  amount: number;
  currency: string;
  senderId: string;
  recipientId: string;
  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>;
}

interface ValidationError {
  code: string;
  message: string;
  field?: string;
  severity: 'error' | 'warning';
}

interface ValidationContext {
  transactionId: string;
  startTime: number;
  metadata: Record<string, unknown>;
}

type SyncValidator = (req: PaymentRequest, ctx: ValidationContext) => ValidationError[];
type AsyncValidator = (req: PaymentRequest, ctx: ValidationContext) => Promise<ValidationError[]>;

interface IValidationRule {
  name: string;
  order: number;
  validate: SyncValidator | AsyncValidator;
}

class ValidationRegistry {
  private rules = new Map<string, IValidationRule>();

  register(rule: IValidationRule): void {
    this.rules.set(rule.name, rule);
  }

  unregister(name: string): boolean {
    return this.rules.delete(name);
  }

  getOrderedRules(): IValidationRule[] {
    return Array.from(this.rules.values()).sort((a, b) => a.order - b.order);
  }
}

class ValidationPipeline {
  constructor(private registry: ValidationRegistry) {}

  async execute(request: PaymentRequest): Promise<ValidationError[]> {
    const context: ValidationContext = {
      transactionId: crypto.randomUUID(),
      startTime: Date.now(),
      metadata: {}
    };

    const allErrors: ValidationError[] = [];

    for (const rule of this.registry.getOrderedRules()) {
      const result = await rule.validate(request, context);
      allErrors.push(...result);
    }

    return allErrors;
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new ValidationRegistry();
registry.register({
  name: 'fraud-score',
  order: 1,
  validate: async (req, ctx) => {
    ctx.metadata['fraudCheck'] = 'passed';
    return [];
  }
});
registry.register({
  name: 'kyc-check',
  order: 2,
  validate: (req, ctx) => {
    return req.senderId ? [] : [{ code: 'KYC001', message: 'KYC verification failed', severity: 'error' }];
  }
});

const pipeline = new ValidationPipeline(registry);
const errors = await pipeline.execute({ amount: 100, currency: 'USD', senderId: 'user1', recipientId: 'user2' });
```

